5465:
Ok, guys, here's a long one!

1) There are major differences between ViperSoft and BRB  1.1
The main program resides in different areas
BRB 1.1 resides in the cassette area (2000 hex to 3FFF hex)
It's variables reside in the 6C20 - 6FFF hex area (see the Blue Ram instructions)
The program areas are from 6000 to 6C1E    and 7000 hex up to (32K) DFFF hex
ViperSoft resides in the 6000-7FFF area even if you are using a cart (there is a koading program in the cartridge to shift it up to it's wirking space, with the variables starting at 8000 hex.
NOONE has ever written a compatibility program between the 2.
To save a program in ViperSoft you SAVE it. If you are pointing to a memory or a string area, you PUT it.
To retrive a program from tape, you LOAD it. If you are putting it into certain memory area os strings, you GET it.  Get it?
There are some other differences. The commands NOT available in ViperSoft are:
 OP (an operator designated ML program)
 Warm boot (in BRB you can hold down the RESET button while simultaneously  holding down the WORDS key (lowest right-hand key) - then releasing the RESET key 1st and finally releasing the WORDS key).,  This  usually gets you to a point  of being the same as hitting the HALT key.
You use this command if your program hangs up, most of the time. Once in a while you won't be able to recover because the machine language part of the program went to never-never land.
I'm not so sure about the PLAY command (Mike is it in Viper?)
There are no 300 baud interface commands in Viper.
I don't know of any other differences. IF there are any, Mike White would know.
That is about the limit of my knowledge here, as I learned this all from Mike White within the last 6 months or so.

Why was the BRB so popular?
Availability. You ordered - you got!   Cheaper!
Versatility. Back in the day, you could get a printer driver (parallel  port, limited handshaking [if any] dot-matrix), a screaming 300 baud modem to use to get on the ever popular bulletin boards, a full keyboard (now totally obsolete because it used a parallel type of interface.), a BSR controller, which allowed you to use your arcade to switch on and off things in the house providing you bought all of the receivers.and an EPROM programmer. Mike White still has a programmer designed for use with the BRB.
Of course ALL of these external things used the Readily Available from Radio Shack the INS8154 wonder chip. Now it is all but extinct.
There are some used ones out there (National Semiconductor quit making them in the early 80's) but they aer expensive and you MUST but a quantity. NO ONE sells onezy-twozy of them.
Another good feature about the Blue Ram was it's small size. It was only slightly larger than the Lil' WHITE RAM. The box, with the ZIF socket on top measured 4.5" x 2.5"  x 1.8" high.
It also came with a Utility (which tested the 8154 and consequently does not run directly from the cart on the Lil WHITE RAM.)
The Utility was for the Modem, Burner, and a Hex Editor and DIS -Assembler. Sorry guys, NO assembler. John Perkins thought I was going to make an assembler, but my knowledge of z-80 at the time was extremely limited.
I hope this helps answer some of the recent questions.
Ken



                  Adam Trionfo wrote:
